## 3. Radio transients from AIC

### AIC from SD systems

AIC in SD systems occurs if the accretion rate onto a WD is large enough to keep the surface burning steady. The required accretion rate is \\sim 10^{-7}-10^{-5}\\ {\\rm M_{\\odot}\\ yr^{-1}}(e.g., Nomoto & Kondo 1991; Nomoto et al. 2007; Shen & Bildsten 2007). There are several SD evolutionary channels for WDs to achieve such a high accretion rate. The high accretion rates are often accompanied with high mass-loss rates, resulting in large CSM density. Thus, the ejecta from AIC collide with the dense CSM and a strong shock emerges. Electrons can be accelerated at the shock and synchrotron emission can be emerged from the accelerated relativistic electrons as found in SNe exploding within CSM (e.g., Fransson & Bjornsson 1998). This synchrotron emission can be observed in radio frequencies.

Let \\dot{M}_{\\rm loss} the mass-loss rate from a SD system leading to AIC. Then, the CSM density \\rho_{\\rm CSM} of the system is expressed as

\\rho_{\\rm CSM}(r)=\\frac{\\dot{M}_{\\rm loss}}{4\\pi v_{w}}r^{-2},  where r is the radius and v_{w} is the wind velocity. We assume that the ejecta expand homologously with the density structure \\rho_{\\rm ej}\\propto r^{-a} (outside) and \\rho_{\\rm ej}\\propto r^{-\\delta} (inside). Then, the forward shock radius (r_{\\rm sh}) and velocity (v_{\\rm sh}) can be obtained analytically as a self-similar solution (Chevalier 1982). We adopt this analytic solution with n=7 and \\delta=1. However, the analytic solution is no longer valid when the reverse shock starts to propagate into the inner flat density region. This starts when the swept-up CSM mass becomes comparable to M_{\\rm ej}, i.e., when M_{\\rm ej}\\sim\\dot{M}_{\\rm loss}r_{\\rm sh}/v_{w}(e.g., Moriya et al. 2013). After this moment, we assume that the shock expands following v_{\\rm sh}=[2E_{\\rm ej}/(M_{\\rm ej}+\\dot{M}_{\\rm loss}r_{\\rm sh}/v_{w})]^{0.5}. Although we change the formulation of v_{\\rm sh} abruptly when M_{\\rm ej}=M_{\\rm loss}r_{\\rm sh}/v_{w} is satisfied for simplicity, v_{\\rm sh} is found to change smoothly. This transition only occurs in the highest CSM density models we show later in this section.

Given the shock radius r_{\\rm sh} and the shock velocity v_{\\rm sh}, the synchrotron luminosity at a frequency \\nu from the shock (L_{\\nu}) can be estimated as (e.g., Bjornsson & Fransson 2004; Fransson & Bjornsson 1998)

\\nu L_{\\nu}\\simeq\\pi r_{\\rm sh}^{2}v_{\\rm sh}n_{\\rm rel,\\gamma_{\\nu}}\\left( \\frac{\\gamma_{\\nu}}{\\gamma_{\\rm min}}\\right)^{1-p}\\gamma_{\\nu}m_{e}c^{2}\\left[ 1+\\frac{t_{\\rm sync,\\nu}}{t}\\right]^{-1},  where n_{\\rm rel,\\gamma} is the number density of the relativistic electrons with the Lorentz factor \\gamma, \\gamma_{\\nu}=(2\\pi m_{e}c\\nu/eB)^{0.5} is the Lorentz factor of the relativistic electrons with the characteristic frequency \\nu, \\gamma_{\\rm min}\\sim 1 is the minimum Lorentz factor of the accelerated electrons, m_{e} is the electron mass, e is the electron charge, and B is a magnetic field strength. We assume that the number density distribution of the relativistic electrons with the Lorentz factor \\gamma follows dn_{\\rm rel,\\gamma}/d\\gamma\\propto\\gamma^{-p} with p=3. The synchrotron cooling timescale at \\nu is t_{\\rm sync,\\nu}=6\\pi m_{e}c/\\sigma_{T}\\gamma_{\\nu}B^{2}, where \\sigma_{T} is the Thomson scattering cross section. We also take the synchrotron self-absorption (SSA) at the shock into account (e.g., Chevalier 1998). The SSA optical depth is \\tau_{\\rm SSA,\\nu}=(\\nu/\\nu_{\\rm SSA})^{-(p+\\alpha)/2} with the SSA frequency \\nu_{\\rm SSA}\\simeq 3\\times 10^{5}(r_{\\rm sh}e/\\epsilon_{e})^{2/7}B^{9/7} Hz in cgs units. Here, \\epsilon_{e} is the fraction of the post-shock energy used for the electron acceleration and \\epsilon_{B} is the fraction converted to the magnetic field energy. We assume \\epsilon_{e}=\\epsilon_{B}=0.1 in the rest of this paper. We neglect the free-free absorption in the unshocked CSM as the CSM density is not high enough for it to be effective.

